The standard approach in .NET is to use the **`System.IO.Abstractions`** NuGet package. It provides an `IFileSystem` interface that wraps all the static `File`, `Directory`, `Path`, etc. calls.

**Steps:**

1. **Install** `System.IO.Abstractions` (and `System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers` for tests).
2. **Inject `IFileSystem`** into your classes instead of calling static methods directly.
3. **In production**, register `FileSystem` (the real implementation) in DI.
4. **In tests**, use `MockFileSystem`.

**Before:**
```csharp
File.WriteAllText(path, content);
var text = File.ReadAllText(path);
if (Directory.Exists(dir)) { ... }
```

**After:**
```csharp
public class MyService
{
    private readonly IFileSystem _fs;
    public MyService(IFileSystem fs) => _fs = fs;

    public void DoWork(string path, string content)
    {
        _fs.File.WriteAllText(path, content);
        var text = _fs.File.ReadAllText(path);
        if (_fs.Directory.Exists(dir)) { ... }
    }
}
```

**In tests:**
```csharp
var fs = new MockFileSystem();
fs.AddFile("/data/input.txt", new MockFileData("hello"));
var svc = new MyService(fs);
svc.DoWork("/data/output.txt", "world");
Assert.True(fs.File.Exists("/data/output.txt"));
```

This avoids writing your own interface/wrapper and gives you a full in-memory file system for testing. Want help applying this to your specific project?
